<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Oleśnica</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Oels</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Oleśnica is a town in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-silesia">Lower Silesia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wroclaw">Wrocław</a>. From around 1320 it was the capital of the Piast Duchy of Oleśnica, which later passed to the Poděbrady family and then to branches of the houses of Württemberg and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brunswick">Brunswick</a>; its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> castle was their residence. Known in German as Oels, it became part of Poland in 1945.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/olesnica.jpg" alt="Oleśnica" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Dawid Galus · CC BY-SA 3.0 pl · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ole%C5%9Bnica_Zamek_02.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Oleśnica lies in the Silesian Lowlands east of the Trzebnickie Hills, on the Oleśnica River, a tributary of the Widawa, about 30 kilometres northeast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wroclaw">Wrocław</a>. It is the administrative seat of Oleśnica County. Its name comes from the Polish word olsza, alder. It stood on an important trade route to Greater Poland, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalisz">Kalisz</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lodz">Łódź</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a>, and it was the site of an important <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printing</a> press and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gymnasium">gymnasium</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>A Piast castle with a nearby abbey and trading settlement is first mentioned in a deed of 1189. On 22 February 1255 Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-iii-the-white">Henry III the White</a> granted the town its privileges, and from the 13th century the area was largely settled by Germans. The town became capital of the Duchy of Oleśnica, partitioned from the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/glogow">Głogów</a>, and Duke Konrad I made it his residence. In 1329 Konrad accepted the overlordship of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lands-of-the-bohemian-crown">Bohemian Crown</a> while keeping wide autonomy. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hussites">Hussites</a> invaded in 1432, and later Polish–Hussite negotiations took place there. The local Piast line ended with the death of Duke Konrad X the White in 1492.</p><p>In 1495 the duchy was sold to Duke Henry I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ziebice">Münsterberg</a>, son of the Bohemian king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-of-podebrady">George of Poděbrady</a>, and his grandson John established a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gymnasium">gymnasium</a> in 1530. When the Podiebrad family died out in 1647 the Swabian dukes of Württemberg inherited the duchy, and in 1792 the Welf dukes of Brunswick-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luneburg">Lüneburg</a>. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-silesian-war">First Silesian War</a> it came under Prussian suzerainty in 1742, and in 1884 the duchy was incorporated into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>. In the 17th century the Polish-German language border ran close to the town, and the Polish writers Adam Gdacius and Jerzy Bock published their works there.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Fighting for the town between 21 and 25 January 1945 and arson afterwards destroyed about 60 to 80 per cent of its buildings. Oleśnica became part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> again after the Potsdam Conference, the remaining German-speaking population was expelled, and the town was resettled with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poles">Poles</a>. The large 16th-century ducal castle, whose inner courtyard arcades are a masterpiece of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> architecture, still marks the town. It also holds the only surviving chained library in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-europe">Central Europe</a>, and its traditional wheat and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rye">rye</a> gingerbread is officially protected.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Oleśnica” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After the Bytom line died out in 1355, the duchy was divided between the dukes of <b>Oleśnica</b> and Cieszyn and later fragmented further, with parts passing among Silesian princes until the end of the 15th century.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-bytom">Duchy of Bytom</a></li><li><blockquote>After him the duchy was divided among branches, including Żagań and <b>Oleśnica</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-glogow">Duchy of Głogów</a></li><li><blockquote>In the division of the Duchy of Głogów among the sons of Henry III, Konrad I received <b>Oleśnica</b> (Oels), east of Wrocław, and ruled it as a separate duchy by 1320.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-olesnica">Duchy of Oleśnica</a></li></ul></section>
